the sea surface is expected to be of lower entity with respect to that of the ship deck, as the sea surface is 15 ft below the ship deck. Again, the complex interactions of the wake with the ship deck and water surface are captured by the wake model, as shown in Figs. 3b and 3d. The recirculation effect from the first example can also be observed by looking at the induced velocity at the rotor disk. Figure 4 shows the contour plot of induced velocities over one revolution for the two examples in consideration. The UH-60 rotor hovering on top of the ship deck (Fig. 4a) shows a higher induced velocity in the fore part of the disk compared to the same rotor in a ship-deck crossing (Fig. 4b). This is an indication that the flow recirculates into the fore part of the rotor due to the interactions with the ship’s superstructure. These results indicate that the combination of a state-space free-vortex wake model and the method of images is suitable for predicting shipboard interaction in approach and landing phases. In fact, the proposed
