C. Runtime Performance
The runtime performance is assessed for each of the simulation models and compared. The analysis is performed using a MATLAB® R2022a implementation of the simulation model and a fourth-order Runge–Kutta solver on a 2022 Mac Studio computer equipped with an Apple M1 Ultra processor. Note that the number of surfaces with which the rotor wake interacts affects the cost of the simulation of the nonlinear dynamics. This is because for each mirror wake/wake portion that is added, the cost of simulation (and linearization) increases as one needs increased Biot–Savart computations. Based on a wake discretization and time step of Δψ Δζ 10 and four rotor wake revolutions [17], the NLTP simulation of the isolated rotor runs approximately 131× slower than real time. When simulating ground effect only (i.e., the second example with the rotor in a shipdeck crossing), only one mirror wake is added, which roughly doubles the runtime of the NLTP simulation compared to that of the isolated rotor. When simulating both ground effect and interaction with one vertical surface (i.e., the first example with the rotor hovering on top of the ship deck and facing the ship superstructure), three mirror wakes are added, which roughly quadruples the runtime of the NLTP simulation compared to that of the isolated rotor. Note that the increase in runtime is not exactly by a factor of four, as the ship superstructure, like the ship deck, is a finite surface that only requires a portion of the rotor wake to be mirrored. Further, because the position of the wake nodes varies with time, they may or may not need to be mirrored depending on whether they are contained in the projection of the ship’s superstructure surface along the direction normal to that surface. While the runtime performance of the nonlinear simulations depends on the factors mentioned above, the runtime performance of the linearized models will only depend on their order. In fact, the linearized models associated with each of these simulations will have the same runtime performance because the nonlinear Biot–Savart calculations are embedded into the time-invariant coefficients of the state-space model. The high-order LTI models have a runtime 87× slower than real time, which is approximately 1.5× faster than the NLTP simulation of the rotor alone. On the other hand, the residualized LTI model runs 1.08× faster than real time while providing very similar predictions to those of the NLTP and high-order LTI models. This is approximately a 142× runtime improvement with respect to the NLTP simulation of the rotor alone, and a 467× runtime improvement compared to the NLTP simulation of the first example. When compared to Euler-based CFD methods [6,36], which were demonstrated to approach real-time speed using massive parallelization (i.e., 2400 cores), the resulting computational cost abatement amounts to approximately three orders of magnitude. It is speculated that by increasing the simulation time step, by further reducing the order of the residualized system, and/or by performing the simulation on a faster machine, faster runtimes may be possible. It is also worth noting that these models are available in Simulink and could be autocoded to C for faster executions. Lastly, the averaged dynamics of the coupled rotor and vortex wake run approximately 2.12× faster than real time. However, it does not retain information on the higher harmonics of the system. These results are reported in Table 1, along
