V. Conclusions
This paper describes the implementation and linearization of a free-vortex wake model in state-variable form and its use in the prediction of complex aerodynamic interactions during shipboard launch and recovery operations. Following a detailed mathematical description, the wake model is implemented for a UH-60 rotor and tested in different flight conditions and for simple control inputs. Interference effects due to the sea surface, ship deck, and ship superstructures are modeled using the method of images. A periodic solution to the wake model is found by time-marching the coupled rotor and vortex wake dynamics. Next, linearized harmonic decomposition models are obtained and validated against nonlinear simulations. Order reduction methods are explored to guide the development of linearized wake models with increased runtime performance compared to nonlinear and linearized harmonic decomposition wake models while guaranteeing satisfactory prediction of the periodic response of the wake. Based on the current work, the following conclusions can be reached: 1) Linearized harmonic decomposition models are shown to accurately predict the nonlinear wake response of a helicopter rotor in a shipboard environment for relatively small amplitude control inputs and for the cases in consideration. More study is required to understand over what amplitude range the linear models will be accurate. While these harmonic decomposition models yield only a slight decrease in simulation runtime when compared to nonlinear dynamics, their order can be conveniently reduced with linear time-invariant model-order reduction methods. 2) Reduced-order models obtained by residualizing the linearized harmonic decomposition models are shown to provide an accurate prediction of the nonlinear wake dynamics at a fraction of the computational cost. 3) Based on the current number of rotor revolutions of wake used, wake discretization, and time step, the coupled nonlinear rotor-wake simulations run approximately 274× slower than real time for when interacting with the ship deck and water surface only, and 430× slower than real time when interacting with the ship deck, ship superstructure, and sea surface. Based on the current number of state, input, and output harmonics retained in the linearized harmonic decomposition models, the simulation of these models run 87× slower than real time. Residualized models run slightly faster than real time, thus providing a two-order-of-magnitude runtime improvement with respect to the nonlinear simulations. The averaged linear dynamics run 2.12× faster than real time but do not capture the harmonic behavior of the response. 4) While the runtime performance of the nonlinear simulations and the cost of linearization largely depend on the amount of interaction with nearby obstacles, the runtime performance of the linearized models will only depend on their order. This is because the added nonlinear Biot–Savart calculations stemming from the image rotor(s) used to model interactions with nearby obstacles become embedded into the time-invariant coefficients of the state-space model.
